.TH STATSTEST 1
.SH NAME
statstest \- check stats(2) against answers worked out by hand
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B statstest
[
.B -v
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Statstest
checks
.IR stats (2)
and prints
.B OK
if everything agrees.
.B -v
prints each measured number.
.PP
Most of what a statistics library does has an exact answer on a small
enough data set, and those are what is used: the eight values 2 4 4 4 5
5 7 9, whose population standard deviation is exactly 2 and whose sample
variance is exactly 32/7; a least-squares line through points that lie
exactly on a line; a polynomial of degree
.IR n \-1
through
.I n
points, which interpolates them.
.PP
The distributions are checked at the values everyone knows \- 1.96 is
the 97.5th percentile of the normal, 2.086 the 97.5th of
.I t
with 20 degrees of freedom \- and against two closed forms:
.I t
with one degree of freedom is the Cauchy distribution, 1/2 +
atan(\fIt\fP)/pi, and with two it is 1/2 +
.IR t /2sqrt(2+ t \u\s-22\s+2\d),
both exact.
The normal quantile is checked by round trip at forty probabilities and
six tail decades, which is stronger than any table: it comes back to
1.1e\-16.
.SS Anscombe's quartet
Four data sets with the same mean, variance, regression line and
correlation and utterly different shapes.
As a test it is unusually good, because the four agreeing is a
coincidence that a wrong formula would break.
The agreement is not exact and was never claimed to be \- the means
agree to two decimal places and the variances to about three \- and the
test asserts that much and no more, since asserting more would be
asserting something about Anscombe's arithmetic rather than about this
library.
.SS Three tests that exist because the first version of them was blind
Each of these replaced a test that passed with the code deliberately
broken.
.PP
The two-pass variance was checked on five values near a
.IR million ,
where every intermediate of the one-pass formula is still a whole number
small enough to hold exactly \- so the one-pass formula gives 2.5 as
well.
At a thousand million it gives exactly
.BR zero .
.PP
The normal cdf was checked to six tail decades, which
.I erf
survives.
At \-10, \-15 and \-20 it does not: 1+erf(\fIx\fP) is one minus a number
that rounds to one, and the answer comes out zero \- a library saying a
ten-sigma event is impossible rather than one in 1.3e23.
.PP
The incomplete beta function has two branches and every
.I t
value tested took the same one, because the continued fraction converges
on the wrong side of its symmetry too, only slowly.
A large number of degrees of freedom with a small
.I t
puts the argument a hair under 1, where 300 iterations are nowhere near
enough: taking the wrong branch returns 0.988 where the answer is
0.5004, and every other check in this program passes while it does.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/statstest.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR stats (2)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Each disagreement prints a line beginning
.B FAIL
naming what was measured, what came out and what was wanted; the count
follows and the program raises an exception.
.SH BUGS
The regression checks all use data that fits the model exactly or nearly
so.
Nothing here has genuinely noisy data with a known true coefficient, so
the standard errors are checked for being zero when they should be and
positive when they should be, but never for being the right size.
.PP
.B Polyfit
is checked to degree 5.
The loss of accuracy the normal equations cause at higher degrees is
described in
.IR stats (2)
and is not measured anywhere.
